# 🔐 Wayback Machine: Recovering Deleted Website Evidence

## Overview

During Day 6 of my security journey, I discovered that a scammer who deleted their website left behind recoverable evidence. Using the Wayback Machine (Internet Archive), I retrieved historical snapshots that served as digital evidence.

## The Problem

Fraudsters and malicious actors often delete their websites after:
- Scamming victims out of money
- Spreading misinformation
- Running phishing campaigns
- Violating laws or regulations

They believe deleting = erasing evidence. **They're wrong.**

## Wayback Machine Methodology

### Step 1: Access the Wayback Machine

```bash
# Navigate to the Internet Archive
https://web.archive.org

# Or use the command line
curl "https://archive.org/wayback/available?url=example.com"
```

### Step 2: Search for Archived Snapshots

```bash
# Using waybackpy (Python library)
pip install waybackpy

# Python script to get snapshots
from waybackpy import WaybackMachineCDXServerAPI
url = "deleted-scam-site.com"
cdx = WaybackMachineCDXServerAPI(url)
for snapshot in cdx.snapshots():
    print(snapshot.archive_url)
```

### Step 3: Retrieve Historical Content

```bash
# Download entire archived site
wget --mirror --convert-links \
  "https://web.archive.org/web/20230101/http://scam-site.com"

# Using wayback-machine-downloader
gem install wayback_machine_downloader
wayback_machine_downloader http://scam-site.com
```

### Step 4: Document & Preserve Evidence

```bash
# Take screenshots with timestamps
screenshot-tool --full-page --timestamp \
  "https://web.archive.org/web/20230101/http://scam-site.com"

# Generate PDF evidence
wkhtmltopdf "archive_url" evidence.pdf

# Hash files for integrity
sha256sum evidence.pdf > evidence.sha256
```

